Day 54 / 60 🩺

Real talk: the last few days of this capstone got technical in a way that wasn't useful to most of you reading this. Git branches, build scripts, schema diagrams. Today I want to show you why any of it mattered.

HealthNexus Command Center now has its first real screen: a single incident queue, ranked by AI, across every facility in a health system. Twenty-four synthetic incidents, from five different facilities, sorted so the highest-risk ones surface first, no matter where they happened. One click narrows it from the whole system down to a single site. It's a small screen, but it's the actual answer to a question every multi-facility COO deals with: severity gets judged differently at every site, and by the time it reaches your desk, the response window has already narrowed.

The part I keep coming back to: this queue ranks incidents by AI, and it will never close one by itself. By Day 57, nothing here can move to "resolved" without a clinician's sign-off, a rule that's already built into HealthNexus's data model today, three days before that screen even exists.

01. A ranked queue only earns trust if the severity language means the same thing at every facility, that consistency is the real product.
02. Letting an executive go from 24 incidents to 7 in one click matters more than any chart.
03. A feature isn't done when it renders. It's done when you've clicked every path, in every language, and watched it hold.

What's one piece of healthcare technology you trust specifically because you know what it won't do, not just what it will?

